      "Language": "spa",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To propose a case-mix methodology for primary care, based on chronicity, type and age. To describe the explanatory value of these variables in the variability of the medical case-load."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Observation, descriptive and retrospective study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Primary care. Rochapea Health Centre, Pamplona."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "Computer records of all the consultations between January 1996 and June 1997. Dependent variable: case-load."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INDEPENDENT VARIABLES",
          "content": "age, type, chronic pathologies (diabetes, lipaemia, chronic neurological diseases, COPD-asthma, chronic psychiatric illnesses, cardiopathy, hypertension, alcohol and other drug abuse). The Kruskal-Wallis test was used to compare work-loads by age groups; and multiple linear regression analysis to calculate the predictive power of the independent variables."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Significant differences were observed for age groups. In the multivariate model used for general practitioners, all the variables could be included. They explained 24.2% of the variability in work load (R2). For paediatricians, age and asthma, explaining 23.48%, could also be included."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Age, type and chronicity are useful variables for predicting case load from administrative data bases. They can be used in adjustments for case load applicable to capitation payment systems."
        }
      ]},
